// ==================================================================================
// MAGIC DOMINO — SITE STRINGS, ALL 17 LANGUAGES
// ==================================================================================
// One table for every page on magicdomino.com. Markup ships ENGLISH and carries
// a key; this swaps it on load. Same mechanism the venue board uses, for the same
// reasons: navigator.language is the reader's own setting and is immune to any cache
// between here and them, and a no-JS reader still gets a complete, usable page.
//
// ⚠️ SEPARATE FROM THE WORKER'S src/i18n.js, DELIBERATELY. That one ships inside the
//    board and landing pages the relay serves; this one ships with GitHub Pages.
//    Different repos, different deploys, no shared build step — so they cannot import
//    each other. The overlap is tiny (a "Watch" button) and duplicating two words
//    beats inventing a build pipeline to share them.
//
// HOW TO USE IT IN MARKUP
//   <h1 data-i18n="hero.sub1">The ultimate dominoes score keeper.</h1>
//   <h2 data-i18n-html="feat.title">Built for <em>real</em> game nights.</h2>
//   <input data-i18n-attr="aria-label:a11y.roomCode">
//
// ⚠️ data-i18n-html EXISTS FOR HEADLINES ONLY, and it is why they are stored as whole
//    sentences with the emphasis inline. Several headlines style one word ("Built for
//    *real* game nights"), and the emphasised word lands in a different place in every language —
//    Spanish puts it last, Japanese restructures the clause entirely. Splitting them
//    into fragments and translating each fragment separately is how you get word
//    salad. Every value here is static content from this file, never user input.
//
// LANGUAGE PRIORITY: ?lang=xx in the URL (ephemeral — for TV links and support
// replies), then the choice saved by the footer switcher (localStorage "mdLang",
// an origin shared with the /tv/ board), then navigator.language, then en. The
// switcher <select class="lang-switch" hidden> ships empty in the markup and is
// populated here with native language names, so it never needs translating and
// stays invisible when JS is off. 404.html has no footer, hence no switcher —
// the URL and saved overrides still apply there.
//
// ⚠️ SEO / <meta> TAGS ARE NOT TOUCHED. A crawler reads the served HTML, which is
//    English, so swapping meta description client-side would fool nobody and help no
//    one. Per-language pages with hreflang would be the real answer to that, and it is
//    a different job from making the page readable.
//
// ── STATUS (2026-09-13): THE SITE IS FULLY LOCALIZED ─────────────────────────────
// All five pages — index.html, 404.html, support.html, privacy.html and
// terms.html — carry keys for every visible string, and every key in this table
// has all 17 languages. terms.html additionally shows a visible notice that the
// English version is authoritative and governs in case of discrepancy, because
// these translations are assistant-written, not professionally reviewed.
//
// ⚠️ A MISSING KEY IS SAFE, NOT BROKEN. apply() skips any element whose key has
//    no entry here, so it keeps the English that ships in the markup. That is why
//    this can be finished incrementally in any order without the site ever
//    showing a blank or a raw key. Never "stub" a key with an English value to
//    make it look complete — that removes the distinction between done and not.
//
// ⚠️ EVERY KEY NEEDS ALL 17 LANGUAGES, and any key whose English contains tags
//    must keep identical tag counts in every language. Both are machine-checkable
//    and were checked on each commit; re-run that check before publishing.
//
// ℹ️ Native-speaker review is still worth doing on ar, ko, bn, hi and ur. These
//    were written by an assistant, not a translator, and at this volume errors
//    are likely to have slipped through.
// ==================================================================================

